
# ── Round 2 renames ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# Staging was indexable while both of these were live, so the old
# URLs may already be in Google. 301, not a delete — a 404 loses
# whatever authority the page had and shows a dead result until
# Google recrawls.

/approach/*   /about/      301!
/press/*      /speaking/   301!

# ── Listing details ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# One physical page (/listings/details/, built from listing-page.html)
# serves every listing — there's no per-listing static file to build,
# since inventory changes without a rebuild. This rewrite (200, not a
# redirect) keeps the pretty /listings/<ListingKey>/ URL in the address
# bar while quietly serving that one page underneath; its script reads
# the key back out of the URL and fetches the real data client-side.
#
# //! CONFIRM — force (the trailing "!") is required: verified live that
# a non-forced 200 rewrite here is silently never applied by netlify
# dev's local redirect engine, even though the equivalent unforced 301
# above works fine.
#
# Netlify's _redirects is evaluated top to bottom, first match wins, and
# a forced rule overrides the "don't shadow a real file" default check
# entirely (confirmed against Netlify's own docs) — so /listings/:key/
# below would otherwise swallow every other real single-segment page
# under /listings/, not just this one. The two rules just below exist
# ONLY to win the match first for those real pages; without them, this
# project's own /listings/map/ page 404'd its way into showing the
# listing-detail template instead of the real map — caught by hand,
# not by anything that would have failed loudly. Any future real page
# added directly under /listings/<one-segment>/ needs the same kind of
# guard added here, in front of the catch-all.
/listings/details/  /listings/details/  200
/listings/map/      /listings/map/      200

/listings/:key/  /listings/details/  200!